# filename: .env.example
# title: Local Configuration Template
# description:
#     Template for the per-developer .env file. Copy this file to ".env"
#     and fill in the real value before running the CLI, the API, or the
#     live test suite. The .env file itself is excluded by .gitignore and
#     must never be committed. This template is safe to commit because it
#     contains placeholders only.
#
# source:
#     PORTED — vendor-evidence-prototype/.env.example
#     Extended with a comment block explaining each variable, so a new
#     contributor can fill the file in correctly without reading the
#     source code.
#
# notes:
#     - SEC requires a descriptive User-Agent header on every request.
#       This is SEC's requirement, not a design choice of this project.
#       Requests without one are rejected with HTTP 403.
#     - The value must identify who is making the request. A name and
#       email address is the convention SEC documents. Example:
#           SEC_API_USER_AGENT=Alexander Cyril alexander.s.cyril@gmail.com
#     - The format is deliberately "Name <email>" or "Name email" —
#       either is accepted by SEC. Use whatever identifies you.
#     - If more configuration variables are added later, document them
#       in this file in the same format so this template stays the
#       complete list of required settings.

# --------------------------------------------------------------------
# Required: SEC User-Agent
# --------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sent with every request to data.sec.gov and www.sec.gov. Without it,
# SEC returns HTTP 403 and no data is retrieved.
#
# Replace the placeholder below with your own name and email address
# after copying this file to ".env".
SEC_API_USER_AGENT=Your Name your_email@example.com